<title>GERMANY: German fin min says no plans to streamline Buba.</title>
<headline>German fin min says no plans to streamline Buba.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1997-07-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>The German Finance Ministry said on Thursday it has no plans to streamline the Bundesbank by reducing the number of its central council members or lowering salaries.</p>
<p>&quot;We currently foresee no changes to the Bundesbank law,&quot; a ministry spokeswoman told Reuters.</p>
<p>The ministry was responding to statements by a Bonn politician in the daily Bild newspaper.</p>
<p>Friedrich Merz, a financial expert for the Christian Democrats (CDU), told Bild that after the scheduled introduction of a single European currency in 1999, there would be no need for such a large number of Bundesbank officials.</p>
<p>He said the government has plans to change the Bundesbank law to reduce the number of council members and employees, cut the length of terms served and reduce pay.</p>
<p>-- Bonn Newsroom, 49 228 26097 150</p>
